WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.336
-Our next guest is a
best-selling author and writer

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.137
who is currently starring in the
third season of

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:07.273
"The Umbrella Academy," which is
streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:08.842
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.110
-What are you? Their mascot?

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:15.782
-More like their ringer.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.349
-Ugh!

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:19.719
Gross, alright?

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:23.489
-Please welcome back to the show
Cazzie David, everybody.

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:26.760
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.305
Cazzie, welcome back!

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.140
Although,
I guess not welcome back.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.442
We've had you on the show,

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:43.843
but you've never been
in the studio.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:46.379
-No, this is my first time
in front of an audience ever.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:47.514
-Wow.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.148
-Yeah, and there's, like,
a lot of them.

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:51.083
-Yeah. You noticed
right away how many,

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:52.418
and there's not even
light on them,

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:54.720
but you can see their
silhouettes and you can tell.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.490
-No, this like
school-play nervous,

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.192
and everyone's my crush.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.628
-I feel like you're you have
a demeanor about yourself that

00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:04.697
people probably can't tell
when you're nervous, though.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.065
-You think so?
-Yeah.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:07.867
I wouldn't guess right now
that you're nervous.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.536
-I, like, asked the driver
to get into an accident

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:11.871
so I didn't have to --

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:12.905
-On the way here?

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.773
So you'd have
a good cover story?

00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:17.076
-Yeah. More, yeah,
just that I could die and not --

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:18.978
-Oh, you wanted to just --
You didn't want to be late.

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.113
You wanted to be a dead person.
-I was like, "Death is fine.

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:23.049
Like, just slow it down."

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.451
-Well, hopefully,
at the end of this,

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:27.987
it will feel like
it was better than dying.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:29.756
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.258
-So, you --
I know you live in L.A.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.561
Your sister, Romy, who I'm
lucky enough to know, as well,

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:36.562
lives in the city.

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:37.797
Do you stay with her?
-Yeah.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:39.799
So, I've been
staying with my sister

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.800
anytime I come to New York.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.372
And, like, the level of
fighting is just unbearable,

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:50.443
and it's about,
like, the tiniest --

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.212
This happened
actually last night.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:55.782
So, I'm in her bedroom, right?

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.383
We're, like, getting ready
to go to sleep.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.484
-Uh-huh.

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:01.955
-And I go to get myself
a glass of water.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:03.189
And then she calls out like,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.157
"Hey, can you get me
a glass of water, too?"

00:02:05.157 --> 00:02:06.292
Which is fine.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.927
Like, "Yeah,
I'm going to get it anyway."

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.161
-Sure.

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.230
-So, I'm getting the water,
and then she goes,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.866
"No, can you come here
and get my glass?"

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.268
-Oh.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:18.905
[ Laughter ]
I know.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.407
By the way,
I live with one of them.

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.809
-Yeah. I know.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.845
-But I'm trying
not to fight with her,

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.246
because I'm
like, "I'm doing 'Seth.'

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.281
I have to have good energy."
-It's her house. Right.

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:31.217
-I just -- I can't take it.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.652
-So, I go into the room

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:36.256
and I hold my hand out
to get her glass.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.191
And then she gets her glass,

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:39.792
which is filled with water,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:43.929
and she proceeds to drink it

00:02:43.929 --> 00:02:49.669
slowly while I'm waiting there.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.237
And then she hands it to me,
and I'm like...

00:02:52.237 --> 00:02:54.307
And that eye roll -- that was
it.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.375
No! That's -- You know what?

00:02:56.375 --> 00:02:59.111
Alright, so,
I'm the eye-roller in my family,

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:00.413
and everybody acts like

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.548
the eye roll
came out of nowhere.

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:05.351
-It's like,
you made the eye roll happen.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.687
-Yeah! The eyes were fine.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.057
They were right here
on that plane,

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:14.260
and then you behaved in a way
that forced them to go on...

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.329
-She said, "Wow,
you can't do anything for me.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:20.500
It was a second.
I was drinking my water."

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.368
Also, there's,
like, water etiquette.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:25.471
Like, if you're going to ask
someone to get you water,

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.373
it has to be
more convenient for them.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:28.507
-Yeah.

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:30.910
-You have to, like,
have a stomachache.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.945
-Yeah. You have to need water.

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:35.347
If they have a water,
you can't --

00:03:35.347 --> 00:03:37.283
If somebody said,
"Bring me a pie,"

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.719
and I came in
and they were eating a pie,

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.587
I'd be like,
"What's wrong with that pie?"

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:43.156
-It's like,
how much do you need?

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:45.324
-How much water do you need?

00:03:45.324 --> 00:03:46.559
-So that was -- Yeah.
-Alright.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.893
-That was one thing.

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:49.962
And it ended with, like,
us truly yelling at each other,

00:03:49.962 --> 00:03:53.365
like, that we wish
the other wasn't even born.

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:55.067
-Well, so that's
a similarity we have.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:58.805
We are -- Sorry.
We live with water tyrants.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.138
-Yeah.

00:04:00.138 --> 00:04:02.041
-Another thing we have in
common, which is pretty cool,

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:05.578
my mother-in-law and your mom
are in a band together.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.246
-Yeah.

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:09.449
-This sounds like a joke,
but it's a true thing.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.550
-It's true.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.052
-It's called --
The name is so good,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.120
I kind of want to give you --

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.688
-It's hard because, like,
I have trouble

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.423
talking about this to,
like, a group of people,

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:18.591
let alone, like, on national TV.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:20.192
-Yeah. Because you're
a little embarrassed

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:21.227
about your mom's success?

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.328
Yeah.

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:24.464
-I'm very embarrassed
about the band.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:30.035
-They are -- They're a bunch of
women who all play the ukulele.

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:34.274
And the name of the band is?
-Is The Ukuladies.

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.844
And it started out as,
like, a sweet hobby.

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:40.045
Like, it was like, "Aww!"

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:43.682
-Nobody was -- Nobody was
a Ukulady hater early on.

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:44.884
-No, it was like, "Nice.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:48.220
You found yourself
a little thing," you know?

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.290
And then they started
recruiting people.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.926
-Yeah.
-And more and more.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.795
-And now there's just
too many Ukuladies.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.464
-And there are so many of them,
and they're out of control.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.199
I feel like we're giving them
too much power

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.500
even talking about them.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.235
-Well, my my wife sent a photo.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.037
She knew you were on
and she was like, "Oh, my God.

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:06.506
You should send this --
You should show this

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:07.639
when Cazzie's on tomorrow."

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:11.009
So, this is a Ukulady rehearsal,
and my daughter,

00:05:11.009 --> 00:05:14.280
my 9-month-old daughter, Addie,
was at rehearsal.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.482
And I feel like Addie
might be on our side

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.318
with how she feels
about these ladies.

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:21.787
So, there's Addie watching.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:23.222
And I really think this --
So, you want to see --

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:24.790
This is the expression
I think I give

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.392
when I walk in
on their rehearsal.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:30.362
-Yeah.

00:05:30.362 --> 00:05:32.231
-It's like that's very much
a face of like,

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.099
"There's a lot of
Ukuladies here."

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:36.969
-No, the most violent thoughts
I've ever had in my life

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.572
has been towards
my mom's ukulele.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:42.374
Like, but I feel like, you know,

00:05:42.374 --> 00:05:45.678
if you know anyone who's over
60, just, like, protect them.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:46.846
They're coming for them.

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.514
-And if you're looking to
book The Ukuladies,

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:49.648
I think they're --

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:51.551
-They're really -- They will.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:52.718
-Go anywhere.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.821
-Yeah.
-They'll do it anywhere.

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.989
-I want to talk about
"Umbrella Academy."

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.991
You know, spoiler alert,
if you haven't caught up,

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:00.093
but I'll just say it.

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:01.126
You died.

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:02.195
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:04.296
-Really quick.
-Yeah.

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:06.299
-Yeah.
-Episode 3?

00:06:06.299 --> 00:06:07.567
-Yeah, quick.
-Yeah.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.435
It wasn't because I was, like --
I mean, it might have --

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.703
because I was so bad,
but, no, that's not why.

00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:13.338
-No, because they write
those scripts in advance.

00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:15.708
They would have had --
-It was written before.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:16.842
-But you died?

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:19.511
-I need to explain this, though.
-Yes.

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:23.716
-I go to the prosthetics team,
and they do a replica

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.584
of your face before you die,
because there's

00:06:25.584 --> 00:06:28.320
going to be
a dummy of me dead.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.223
And I said to them -- I said,

00:06:31.223 --> 00:06:34.059
"You know, if you guys
could just do me, like, a solid

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:38.964
and make me look, like, on the
prettier side of dead, then --"

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:39.998
-Very nice.
-Yeah.

00:06:39.998 --> 00:06:42.301
Like, just, like,
subtle improvements.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:45.170
-So that when people see
the dead version of you,

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:46.772
they're going to go like,
"Ay-yi-yi!"

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:48.807
-They'll be like,
"Oh, like, this young, pretty,

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:52.211
like, girl has perished,"
like a Cinderella.

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.080
-Yeah. Where you would feel
great empathy and loss

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.216
by the beauty that has
been taken from us too soon.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.350
-Correct.
-Yeah.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.718
And do you feel like
they did a good job?

00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:01.086
-So, that's the thing.

00:07:01.086 --> 00:07:03.322
I said this to them.
I kind of charmed them.

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.825
I tried to make sure
they would do it.

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:08.127
They were like, "Okay."

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:11.330
And then I -- You know, a little
bit of the work is on me,

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.932
because they are
replicating my face.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:15.134
And they put the mask on me,
so I tried to look pretty.

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:16.468
I was like --
-Right.

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:18.237
So while -- Oh, yeah, you were
doing -- Oh, that's good.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:19.605
So that was the face.
-For like two hours.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.107
-Okay, show us how.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:22.241
Okay, great.

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.276
-You know, like, pretty.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:27.246
That was very -- That position
is definitely, like, your best.

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:28.513
-Yeah.

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:31.684
So, like two weeks go by,
and my friend sends me

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:35.020
a picture of the -- He says,

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.023
"Yo, like,
your prosthetic's done."

00:07:37.023 --> 00:07:39.491
And I said, "Oh, my God.
Like, how do I look?"

00:07:39.491 --> 00:07:43.562
And this is the image
he sent me.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:47.066
[ Laughter ]

00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:48.401
And there's just,
like, a lot like.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:51.604
Like, so, first of all,
like, obviously,

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:54.340
like, they're a
talented prosthetics team.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.574
-Yeah. They know what
they're doing.

00:07:55.574 --> 00:07:57.009
-But they also hate me.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:01.513
Like, it's, like, not even,
like, the worst-case scenario.

00:08:01.513 --> 00:08:04.349
Like, my imagination wasn't even
good enough for, like,

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:05.451
the worst-possible --

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:07.886
-Yeah. Now, I will say,
in their defense,

00:08:07.886 --> 00:08:09.555
they gave you a bangin' body.

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:15.227
-Yeah. No, that's -- And it was
like, anywhere I went,

00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:17.630
it was just, like,
sitting there kind of.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.798
-And then, I should note,
it was on -- Your --

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:21.834
The dead version of you
is on the show a lot.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:24.070
-It's more than me alive.
-Yeah.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.138
Because they, like,
keep cutting to you dead.

00:08:26.138 --> 00:08:28.040
So, like,
people in the end might, like --

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.577
Their memory of you on the show
might be like this version.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:33.479
-I was shocked
while watching it,

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:36.382
because I was just like,
"Oh, there's more

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:37.850
of this than me."

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.686
-You did get to hang out with
the dead version of yourself,

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:43.189
and you guys seem to be having
a super-fun time over there.

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:44.257
Yeah.

00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:46.659
-I took this
because I was so angry.

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.529
Yeah, so --
-Alright.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:52.131
-But the self -- like, the --

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:54.733
what that photo
has done to my self-image,

00:08:54.733 --> 00:08:57.169
like, that's what I see
when I think of

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.537
what I look like.

00:08:58.537 --> 00:09:01.641
And I actually am -- When I pass
by a mirror, I'm relieved.

00:09:01.641 --> 00:09:05.778
Like, I'm like, "Oh, my God.
I'm actually, like, passable.

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.079
Like, it's fine.

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:10.516
-You're more than passable.
You're very beautiful.

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:11.717
The face you made is --

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:13.152
Obviously, you should do that
one all the time.

00:09:13.152 --> 00:09:15.487
-Yeah.
-That's the best one.

00:09:15.487 --> 00:09:16.522
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:17.723
It's always such
a delight to see you.

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.925
-Thank you for having me.
-Cazzie David, everybody.

00:09:19.925 --> 00:09:21.327
The third season of
"The Umbrella Academy"

00:09:21.327 --> 00:09:22.528
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:23.529
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

